\details{
The SAS \verb{\%summarytable} macro marks every p-value with a superscript
letter naming the test behind it. This builds those footnotes from the
\code{test_name} column \code{\link[gtsummary:add_p]{gtsummary::add_p()}} records, in the format
\code{\link[=hv_man_table_save_jtcvs]{hv_man_table_save_jtcvs()}}'s \code{footnotes} argument expects, so you do
not have to map test identifiers or count body rows by hand.

Takes the \code{gtsummary} object rather than the \code{flextable}, because
\code{\link[=hv_man_table_jtcvs]{hv_man_table_jtcvs()}} discards \code{test_name} when it reshapes the body.
The returned \code{row} indices are rendered body rows, counting the
section-header rows \code{\link[=hv_man_table_jtcvs]{hv_man_table_jtcvs()}} interleaves, and are
therefore valid only against that renderer's output.

Letters follow a fixed order (Wilcoxon, Kruskal-Wallis, chi-square,
Fisher), filtered to the tests actually used, so two tables in the same
manuscript that use the same tests get the same letters regardless of
variable order. Every row sharing a test collapses onto one letter.

Returns an empty list when there is nothing to mark: \code{by = NULL},
\code{compare = "none"}, or \code{compare = "smd"}, since a standardized
difference is not a test. An empty list concatenates and renders
harmlessly, so no conditional is needed at the call site.

With \code{compare = "both"} the cell reads e.g. \code{0.7 (SMD -0.03)} and the
letter marks the p-value portion of it. Note also that
\code{\link[gtsummary:add_difference]{gtsummary::add_difference()}} requires exactly two groups, so
\code{compare = "smd"} and \code{compare = "both"} are unavailable for a 3-group
table; \code{compare = "pvalue"} is.

An unrecognized \code{test_name} (only reachable from a hand-built object
that passed \code{add_p(test = ...)}, since \code{\link[=hv_tbl_summary]{hv_tbl_summary()}} never sets
\code{test}) is kept, using the raw identifier as its footnote text, and
warned about. A marked cell whose letter has no definition would be
worse than an unpolished label.
}
\section{Common mistakes}{

\strong{"\code{tbl} must be a gtsummary table object."} You passed the
\code{flextable}. This needs the \code{gtsummary} object, because
\code{\link[=hv_man_table_jtcvs]{hv_man_table_jtcvs()}} drops the \code{test_name} column when it reshapes
the body -- by render time the test identities are gone. Pass the
same object you passed to the renderer.

\strong{An empty list back, and no footnotes in the document.} Expected,
not a failure: nothing is marked when \code{by = NULL},
\code{compare = "none"}, or \code{compare = "smd"}, since a standardized
difference names no test. An empty list concatenates and renders
harmlessly, so the call site needs no conditional.

\strong{Row indices that land on the wrong rows.} They are computed from
\code{tbl$table_body} at the moment you call this, and they count the
section-header rows \code{\link[=hv_man_table_jtcvs]{hv_man_table_jtcvs()}} interleaves, so they are
valid against that renderer's output and nothing else. Finish the
table first and call this last.

\strong{Passing the result to \code{\link[=hv_man_table_save]{hv_man_table_save()}}.} This is the JTCVS
shape. The CORR saver takes a symbol-keyed list and rejects it; its
equivalent is \code{\link[=hv_man_footnotes]{hv_man_footnotes()}}.
